<title>UK: UK's Clarke-EMU may not start on schedule in 1999.</title>
<headline>UK's Clarke-EMU may not start on schedule in 1999.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-10-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Chancellor of the Exchequer Kenneth Clarke said on Thursday he did not think a European single currency would begin as scheduled on January 1, 1999.</p>
<p>Speaking on a BBC television show, Clarke said the British government would decide whether to enter the single currency based on the national interest at the time.</p>
<p>Clarke, speaking after his speech to the Conservative party conference, said it was too early to say how plans for a single currency would develop or even which countries would join. &quot;I don't think they will go the first of January 1999,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>-- London Newsroom 0171 542 7947</p>
